\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Definition}
\title{The flux of a closed plaquette surface}
\date{2026-11-26}

\p{Let #{n\ge 2}, let #{N_j\ge 3}, and let #{U} be a unitary connection on a
finite-dimensional space #{F}. For #{i<j} the oriented plaquette #{p=(x;i,j)} is traversed
from #{x} to #{x+e_i}, to #{x+e_i+e_j}, to #{x+e_j} and back to #{x}. The number
##{u_p=\det U_p=\frac{\det U_i(x)\,\det U_j(x+e_i)}{\det U_i(x+e_j)\,\det U_j(x)}}
is the determinant of the \vocabk{plaquette holonomy}{fdip-006N} #{U_p}, a complex number of
modulus #{1}. For #{F=\mathbb C} it is the product of the links around #{p}. The reversed
plaquette #{-p} has #{u_{-p}=u_p^{-1}}. The plaquette is \newvocab{plain} when
#{u_p\ne -1}, and then its \newvocab{plaquette angle} #{\varphi_p\in(-\pi,\pi)} is the
argument of #{u_p}, with #{\varphi_{-p}=-\varphi_p}. A \newvocab{closed plaquette surface} is
an integer combination #{c=\sum_pc_p\,p}, using one orientation of each plaquette as a basis, whose boundary, a combination
of oriented links, is zero. When every plaquette with #{c_p\ne 0} is plain, the
\newvocab{plaquette flux} of #{c} is
##{\operatorname{fl}(c)=\frac{1}{2\pi}\sum_pc_p\varphi_p.}}
